# Error Handling

- Handle errors and edge cases close to the start of the function that can detect them.
- Use shared error handlers from `app/common/exceptions/handlers.py` and shared error responses from `app/common/responses/error_response.py`. Do not hand-build `HTTPException.detail` dictionaries or invent local error payload shapes.
- Use specific HTTP status codes and stable client-facing messages.
- Preserve structured error details for fields, retry state, blocking state, or presentation hints when the API already has a schema for them.
- Log unexpected operational failures where they can be diagnosed, but do not log secrets, OTPs, bearer tokens, refresh tokens, password material, or raw authorization headers.
- Do not swallow exceptions after rollback, cleanup, or logging. Re-raise unless the caller receives an intentional replacement error.
